No direct number. From community knowledge: AO walking is tile-by-tile with a fixed walk interval; the classic value used across AO engines (AO Libre, Imperium) is about 400ms per tile ("velocidad de caminata 400"). In AO Libre source, `WALK_SPEED`-ish constant... The widely referenced figure: characters move ~2.5 tiles/second (400 ms per tile). I'll set STEP_DURATION = .4 with a comment citing AO Libre. If it feels slow, easy to tune; the user asked to match the classic mechanic and explicitly rejected 0.13.

Also they want NOBODY standing: 
- Remove aggro drop-stand (always chase nearest enemy).
- No-target case: enemies all dead → game over state? There's always enemies (both teams 4v4). But between respawns: if all enemies dead, walk home. Add home-return step.
- The aggro gate currently: `if (!target || distance > NPC_AGGRO_RANGE) { ...aiTimer = rand(.3,.8); continue; }` → replace: target always chase; if !target (no alive enemies) → move home via nextPathStep toward {homeX, homeY}.

Implementation:
```js
      const target = tacticalEnemy(actor);
      // Nadie se queda quieto: con blanco lo persigue (el agite nunca para); sin blancos vivos vuelve a su puesto.
      if (!target) {
        if (!actor.movePlan || actor.movePlan.kind !== 'home') {
          const homeStep = nextPathStep(actor, { x: actor.homeX, y: actor.homeY }, 0, 2);
          if (homeStep) startStep(actor, homeStep[0], homeStep[1]);
        }
        actor.aiTargetId = null;
        continue;
      }
      actor.aiTargetId = target.id;
```
Wait — nextPathStep(actor, targetLike, minRange, maxRange): BFS toward tile, goal when distance in [min,max] → with 0..2 it stops within 2 of home ✓. `actorTile({x,y})` works (no .step). But careful: nextPathStep reads `target` only for [tx, ty] ✓.

Also chaining: movePlan not set for home — single steps per tick (tick .12-.2, step .4) → walking home continuously? Each tick tries a step; if already stepping → loop top `if (!actor.alive || actor.step) continue` skips → next tick after step done, aiTimer may be >0... hmm aiTimer decrements every frame (not gated by step) — while stepping, `continue` skips aiTimer decrement? Loop: `if (!actor.alive || actor.step) continue;` — yes aiTimer freezes while stepping. After step completes, tick resumes; aiTimer likely ≤0 → decides again → steps home continuously ✓ (tick fires because aiTimer was counting down between steps... wait no — aiTimer only decrements when NOT stepping. While stepping (0.4s), aiTimer frozen at ≤0? It was decremented to ≤0 before starting the step? Sequence: aiTimer hits 0 → tick → home step started (actor.step set) → subsequent frames skip (stepping) → step done → next frame: aiTimer still ≤0 → tick again → next step ✓ continuous.

Remove `const recovering`? No — keep: it gates casts during castCd. But now with "recovering" NPCs still move via the normal movement section ✓.

Also remove NPC_AGGRO_RANGE constant + the gate block. Replace gate with the no-target/home logic above.

Also the aggro gate removal: casts only within SPELL_VISION_RANGE ✓ (all aim blocks check distance ≤ 11). Chasing beyond vision = just walking ✓.

STEP_DURATION .4 → also affects position() interpolation for all ✓. Player walk = classic AO ✓.

Also npcPlanStep chain pause rand(.05,.16) — with 0.4s steps fine.

Also — "si te quedas quieto = morir" also implies the PLAYER should keep moving; that's the player's choice. ✓.

Another standing source: melee fallback requires `range <= 1` and attackCd; if warrior adjacent and attackCd>0 but NOT recovering → movement section: range in band → strafe ✓ dancing.

But careful: warrior strafes while target adjacent — nextStrafeStep scores tiles at distance 1 from target — if all adjacent tiles of actor lead to distance 2 (blocked), no step → stands → next tick retries ✓ acceptable (blocked corners).

Also with always-chase, distance for mages: band 4-6 → mages approach to 6 then dance ✓.

One more: support 'cleanse'/'heal' aims: aim targets allies — beginNpcAim plants caster briefly ✓.

